{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 0,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s hard to interpret the strange results\nof quantum mechanics, though many have tried."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3840,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Interpretations range from the outlandish—like\nthe multiple universes of Many Worlds, to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8460,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the almost mundane, like the very mechanical\nPilot Wave Theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12060,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But perhaps we’re converging on the answer, "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14400,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because some are arguing that \nthese two interpretations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17940,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are really the same thing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24960,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we have perfect knowledge of the universe\nat any point, we can plug that knowledge into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29640,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the equations of the laws of physics and determine\nthe exact state of the universe at any other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34320,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time in the past or future."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35640,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At least according to classical physics, in\nwhich the world is represented as particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40380,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and fields that obey the equations of physical\nlaw lock-step."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45000,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Classical physics is what we call deterministic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48660,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But then along came quantum mechanics and\nmessed up everything."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52740,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In quantum mechanics, we represent the world\nnot as particles and fields, but as wavefunctions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57900,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are abstract mathematical objects that\nencode the physical state of a system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62700,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And like a good physics object wavefunction\nevolves deterministically—in this case under"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67500,
    "dDurationMs": 1020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Schrodinger equation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68520,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the wavefunction alone doesn’t tell\nyou what you’ll actually see if you try"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72360,
    "dDurationMs": 900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to make a measurement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73980,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That action seems to introduce randomness\ninto our deterministic theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78600,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Take the famous double slit experiment, for\nexample."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80880,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We send a particle—say a photon— via a\npair of slits to land at a single spot on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85560,
    "dDurationMs": 540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a detector."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86100,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we send photon after photon they’ll guide\nup a series of bands identical to the interference"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92640,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pattern that you’d get by sending not a\nsingle particle, but a wave that traverses"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97020,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both slits."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98640,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The quantum wavefunction behaves exactly like "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101220,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "such a wave, and its evolution \nunder the Schrodinger"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104100,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equation accurately predicts the shape of\nthe interference pattern we see."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107220,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, it doesn’t seem to predict the\nfirst and most obvious result of this experiment"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111480,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "– that each photon lands at a single spot\non the detector."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115020,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That is, the quantum wavefunction stays wave-like\nwhen it gets to the detector, but we don’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120840,
    "dDurationMs": 840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observe this wave."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121680,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We only observe a localized particle striking\nthe detector at a single location that seems"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127380,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be picked randomly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130140,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This apparent randomness is a big deal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132600,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It suggests that Nature is NOT deterministic\nafter all—that we can’t perfectly predict"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137700,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the future or recover the past simply by knowing\nthe present."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141120,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, sometimes things that look random aren’t\nreally random—they look that way because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145140,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we don’t have all the information."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146100,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, flipping a coin only seems random\nbecause we’re not smart enough to calculate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150540,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exactly how the coin will spin before it lands."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152880,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the non-determinism of quantum mechanics\nis taken to be fundamental—at least by the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159180,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dominant interpretation of the theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161700,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Copenhagen interpretation states that\nreality is fully described by the wavefunction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166560,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evolving under the Schrodinger equation, and\nthat observation or measurement “collapses"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171300,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the wavefunction” in a way that is truly\nrandom."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174600,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Copenhagen became the default interpretation,\nor “story behind the math” despite some"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179820,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fundamental problems and despite opposition\nby the likes of Einstein and even Schrodinger himself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185520,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And some physicists objected enough \nto seek alternative interpretations of quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191160,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mechanics—and some interpretations might save determinism\nin the process."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197520,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The two most famous alternatives to the Copenhagen\ninterpretation are Pilot Wave Theory and the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202200,
    "dDurationMs": 1020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many Worlds Interpretation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203220,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the surface they seem as different as you\ncan imagine."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205800,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pilot Wave theory is almost mundane, while\nMany Worlds feels more out-there than Copenhagen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212880,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And yet there are deep underlying connections\nbetween these two ideas that make them much"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217860,
    "dDurationMs": 1080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more similar than you’d think."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218940,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To the point that some proponents of Many-Worlds\nclaim that Pilot Wave Theory is really just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224520,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many-Worlds “in a state of chronic denial”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227160,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve talked about both pilot wave theory\nand many worlds before, and those episodes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231840,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are worth a look for more detail on the individual\ninterpretations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235560,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today’s episode is about comparing them,\nand perhaps to see which is the more worthy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240720,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "successor to Copenhagen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243480,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we dig into the alternate interpretations,\nlet’s say a bit more about what’s wrong"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247800,
    "dDurationMs": 1260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with Copenhagen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249060,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For one thing, the Schrodinger equation tells\nus how the wavefunction evolves, but it doesn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253920,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "directly give us the probability of getting\na particular result when we make a measurement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257880,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That requires something called the Born rule,\nwhich just says that you square the wavefunction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263520,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to get the probability distribution of the possible measurement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267840,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Copenhagen doesn’t tell us where that\nsquaring comes from—it’s just accepted"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272700,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because it works."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274020,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another problem is that the collapse of the\nwavefunction has to happen across the entire"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278700,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wavefunction instantaneously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280260,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the photon is detected at this spot on\nthe detector then the chance of it appearing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285120,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anywhere else becomes instantly zero."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287220,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This violates something called “locality”,\nand implies a sort of faster-than-light influence,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292620,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which on its surface violates the core axiom\nof Einstein’s relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296820,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, do the alternatives do any better?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299580,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start with pilot wave theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301680,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This was first proposed by Louis deBroglie\nand then fully developed by David Bohm."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307020,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The idea is that the wavefunction behaves\nexactly as in standard quantum mechanics,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311280,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but that it’s not really the wavefunction\nthat is being directly measured."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314460,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rather, the wavefunction acts to guide the\nmotion of an actual particle, which we’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318540,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "call a corpuscle to avoid confusion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321000,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A “entire” particle, like a photon, is\na corpuscle riding in its wavefunction, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327000,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’ll start calling the guiding wave when\nwe’re referring to pilot wave theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331620,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the double slit experiment, the guiding\nwave goes through slits and interferes with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335820,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "itself to produce the classic interference\npattern."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338280,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we don’t measure the guiding wave, only\nthe corpuscle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341700,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the corpuscule only goes through one slit,\nriding the wave and being deposited on the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347160,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detector at a single spot, although that location\nis influenced by the interference pattern."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353520,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The guiding wave for consecutive particles\nmight look exactly the same to us, but the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359520,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "corpuscule riding each can take different\npaths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363120,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After many of them they’ll map out the shape\nof the guiding wave at the detector."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367560,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But how does each corpuscule know which path\nto take if it’s not random?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372300,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In Pilot Wave Theory, the answer to that is\nthe same as the answer to why does a coin"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377400,
    "dDurationMs": 2116,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "land heads versus tails."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379516,
    "dDurationMs": 4844,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's that there's information that we aren’t aware of that defines the detailed path—information"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384360,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s not in the guiding wave alone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386160,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That makes Pilot Wave Theory a so-called hidden\nvariable theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390540,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In Pilot Wave Theory the hidden information\nis in the form of the exact position of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395220,
    "dDurationMs": 4374,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "corpuscule, and that isn’t coded in the\nwavefunction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399594,
    "dDurationMs": 5526,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We also need to add something called the guiding\nequation, which helps the corpuscule pick"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405120,
    "dDurationMs": 1140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out a particular path."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406260,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That depends on the wavefunction and on the position of not only the corpuscule in question,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411960,
    "dDurationMs": 5247,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but also on every other corpuscule in the\nuniverse that this system is entangled with."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417207,
    "dDurationMs": 1293,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we’ll come back to that point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418500,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So in the Pilot Wave picture, the “world”\nis made of these corpuscules being pushed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424020,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around by these guiding waves under the omniscient\noverview of the guiding equation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428880,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s a bit like how the electromagnetic\nfield can push around an electron, but with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434040,
    "dDurationMs": 900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an enormous difference."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434940,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When an EM field shoves an electron, the electron\nshoves back."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439320,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For pretty much every type of particle and "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441420,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field interaction there’s \nthis sort of back-reaction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444720,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Except for pilot waves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446160,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The guiding wave is completely unaffected\nby the corpuscle—and this is necessary if"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452220,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we want the Schrodinger equation to be strictly\nobeyed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455340,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The guiding wave will channel the corpuscule\nand the guiding wave will interact with itself"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460260,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and with the guiding wave of other particles,\nbut it completely ignores the presence or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465180,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "absence of the corpuscule within it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467700,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is going to be extremely important in\na minute."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470400,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if we know the initial positions of all\nthe corpuscles we could use wavefunction to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476160,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "predict the result of any experiment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478920,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In pilot wave theory the apparent randomness\nof quantum mechanics is not fundamental, but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484680,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "instead emerges from our lack of knowledge\nabout the position of all the corpuscles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490080,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So se’ve now solved the non-determinacy problem\nof Copenhagen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494160,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve also solved the question of what causes\nthe collapse of the wavefunction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497760,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It doesn’t need to collapse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500100,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In Pilot Wave theory the parts of the guiding\nwithout the corpuscle just continue on because,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507120,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "remember, the corpuscle has no effect on it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Presumably we can ignore this empty guiding\nwave because we only detect the corpuscle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515520,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s now look at what happens at the detector\nwhen the photon wavefunction arrives."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522120,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For both Copenhagen and pilot wave theory\nthe photon wavefunction reaches the screen"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527220,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and encounters electron wavefunctions in the\ndetector pixels."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532020,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "An electron wavefunction in one of those pixels\nis excited, nudging electrons further down"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538320,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the circuit pathway so that eventually a photon\ngets emitted by a computer screen indicating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543720,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which pixel was excited, and so recording\nthe arrival location of the photon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549360,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what happens to the photon wavefunction\neverywhere else on the detector?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554940,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, we know that it's possible for electrons\nto be in a state of simultaneously excited"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559560,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and not excited—what we call a superposition\nof states."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562680,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That would also happen if, for example, we'd\nplaced a single electron inside one of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568080,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the slits."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569580,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the photon can be in a superposition of\npassing through both slits, then that electron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575160,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enters a superposition of being both excited\nand not excited."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579300,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's true for Copenhagen and for pilot wave theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582900,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the latter case, the corpuscle of the photon\nreally does pass through one slit or the other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588000,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If it passes through the slit with the electron,\nthen the corpuscle of that electron follows"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594420,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the excited part of the electron wavefunction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597720,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the photon corpuscle passes through the\nother slit, then the electron wavefunction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602280,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is excited by an empty segment of the photon\nwavefunction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607660,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the guiding equation then forces the electron corpuscle to remain in the unexcited part"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612480,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of its wavefunction, while the excited part\nof the electron wavefunction remains empty."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617340,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Same at the detector."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619680,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Electrons in all pixels enter a superposition "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622740,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of excited and not excited \nas the photon wavefunction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626340,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reaches all points on the detector."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628560,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the part of these electron wavefunctions "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631080,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that become excited will \nin turn excite neighboring"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633720,
    "dDurationMs": 1020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electron wavefunctions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634740,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All across the detector, this superposition\nshould propagate through the circuitry--a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639540,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cascade of interactions that leads to a record\nof the photon landing at that pixel and at"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645840,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same time silence from that pixel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649020,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So at least for a little bit, the world is\nin a complex superposition of each pixel being"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654540,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "excited and not."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657120,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course we only observe a signal from one\npixel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659880,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, what happens to the cascades from all\nof the others?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662580,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, Copenhagen would tell us that somewhere\nbetween the photon arriving at the detector"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667560,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and us becoming aware of the signal from the\nscreen, all of the extraneous superpositions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672780,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "die off, leaving only one excited pixel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675780,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The wavefunction collapses."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677580,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However pilot wave theory doesn't collapse\nthe wavefunction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681000,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "IT would say that an electron wavefunction\nin all pixels does enter a superposition of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685860,
    "dDurationMs": 1200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "excited and not excited."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687060,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But all of those excited states are empty\nof corpuscules except for one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692760,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wherever the photon corpuscle arrives, that\nelectron corpuscle follows the excited electron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699240,
    "dDurationMs": 720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wavefunction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The cascade of excitations from that one pixel\nall carry corpuscles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705960,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we end up with this interesting situation\nwhere we have many similar cascades of excitation,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710820,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but only one of them is \"real\" because it\ncarries corpuscles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714240,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The rest are sort of \nphantoms--empty superpositions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717660,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In pilot wave theory, the \ncascade of superpositions "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721200,
    "dDurationMs": 3430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doesn't end at the detector, nor at the computer screen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724630,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead, we end up with a superposition of\nour brain in many states--each corresponding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729600,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to an awareness of the photon landing in a\ndifferent spot."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732120,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But only one of these is \"real\" because it\ncarries corpuscles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736980,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The rest are the phantom, corpuscle-empty\nwavefunctions of our brains as they would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743400,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have looked if the particle landed in other\nlocations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746520,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is probably a good time to get to the\nMany Worlds Interpretation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751500,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fortunately I don’t have to describe how\nour double-slit experiment looks in Many Worlds"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756540,
    "dDurationMs": 1380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because I just described it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757920,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Take pilot wave theory and just subtract the\ncorpuscles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761760,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The corpuscles served as markers of which\nof these diverging alternate realities was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767640,
    "dDurationMs": 1260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the real one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768900,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the corpuscles themselves have no influence\non the wavefunction, so they really are just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774660,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "labels."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776100,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Without the corpuscle, all the branches of\nthe wavefunction are empty—all are ghosts,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781080,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or perhaps none of them are."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783300,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The wavefunction in pilot wave theory and\nin many worlds behaves exactly the same way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788460,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that includes not collapsing, and not\nbeing random."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792120,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The main difference between the two is the\nexistence or not of the corpuscules."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795960,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In pilot wave theory, corpuscules mark real\nphysical reality, while many worlds accepts"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802320,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the wavefunction itself is the primary and really sole elementary constituent of physical reality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809100,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another difference is the guiding equation\nin pilot wave theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812220,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many worlds doesn’t need it because the\nevolution of the wavefunction alone only needs"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816600,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Schrodinger equation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818100,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This guiding equation presents its own issues."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820800,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The main one is that it needs to know about\nnot just the corpuscle in question, but also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825960,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every other corpuscle connected to it via\nentanglement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828960,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It needs this to ensure that all corpuscles\nare always funneled into the one “real”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833940,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "world."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834900,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This means that the Guiding Equation is explicitly\nnon-local, causing instantaneous interactions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841500,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between corpuscles across arbitrarily large\nregions of space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844920,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, we now know that it’s impossible\nfor any hidden variable theory, like Pilot Wave Theory, to preserve"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852720,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "locality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854340,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because of this no one has managed to make "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856500,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pilot wave theory play nice \nwith special relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859560,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However it’s easy enough to make our theory\nrelativistic if we just delete the corpuscles—but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866220,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then of course we have Many Worlds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868320,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Occam’s razor tells us that the simplest\nexplanation is usually the most correct."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872760,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Don’t add parameters to your model if they\naren’t needed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 875700,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some have said that the branching timelines\nof Many Worlds is an addition that goes against"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880980,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Occam’s razor—but proponents of Many Worlds\nsay that these timelines are a core prediction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 885900,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of raw quantum mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887820,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To get rid of them you have to add something:\neither the collapse of the wavefunction in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892500,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Copenhagen, or the tagging of the “one true\nworld” with corpuscles in pilot wave theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899340,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s ask one more question: do any of these\ninterpretations explain the Born rule?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904920,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know that Copenhagen just takes it on faith\nthat you need to square the wavefunction to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909180,
    "dDurationMs": 1677,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get the probabilities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910857,
    "dDurationMs": 3663,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Do they fall out more naturally in pilot wave\ntheory or many worlds?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914520,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, in pilot wave theory the probability\nof getting a measurement in a particular location"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919380,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "depends on the distribution of corpuscles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922080,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turns out that if you start the universe\nwith the corpuscules distributed according"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926940,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the Born rule—so their density being "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929160,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proportional to the square \nof the wavefunction—then"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931980,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at all later times they’ll \nretain that distribution, "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934800,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so the Born rule will continue to work."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937740,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But pilot wave theory doesn’t tell us how\nthe corpuscles got distributed that way in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 942780,
    "dDurationMs": 480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first place."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 943260,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In many worlds, the probability of recording\na particular measurement value is the probability"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948660,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of you finding yourself in a timeline where\nthat value actually happened."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it’s about the density of timelines—how\nmany alternate realities correspond to your"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957960,
    "dDurationMs": 360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measurement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958320,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the fact is, you CAN recover the born\nrule with this sort of statistics of alternate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963660,
    "dDurationMs": 780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "worlds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964440,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can calculate the probability of landing\nin one particular world."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967740,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the probability that you will learn how to do this close to 100%, as long as your"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 974160,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum timeline includes a near-future episode\nof Space Time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone. We wanted to let you know about our \nPatreon. It’s an incredibly helpful way to support  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983880,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this show and ensure our survival long-term. If \nyou join there’s lots of amazing perks including  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989280,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "monthly Q\u0026A livestreams, access to episode \nscripts, and even a private chat with me depending  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994680,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
